{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01B8Y6ovERkp8ctj7k2CB4kA","type":"tool_result","content":"# ACL Rolling Review Scoring Scales\n\n## (1) Overall Assessment/Recommendation\n- **5** = \"Consider for Award\" (top 2.5% papers)\n- **4.5** = Borderline Award\n- **4** = Conference acceptance\n- **3.5** = Borderline Conference\n- **3** = Findings acceptance\n- **2.5** = Borderline Findings\n- **2** = Resubmit next cycle\n- **1.5** = Resubmit after next cycle\n- **1** = Do not resubmit\n\n## (2) Soundness\n- **5** = \"Excellent: This study is one of the most thorough I have seen\"\n- **4.5** = (intermediate)\n- **4** = \"Strong: This study provides sufficient support for all claims\"\n- **3.5** = (intermediate)\n- **3** = \"Acceptable: sufficient support for main claims\"\n- **2.5** = (intermediate)\n- **2** = \"Poor: main claims not sufficiently supported\"\n- **1.5** = (intermediate)\n- **1** = \"Major Issues: not sufficiently thorough to warrant publication\"\n\n## (3) Excitement/Novelty\n- **5** = \"Highly Exciting: I would recommend this paper to others\"\n- **4.5** = (intermediate)\n- **4** = \"Exciting: I would mention this paper to others\"\n- **3.5** = (intermediate)\n- **3** = \"Interesting: might mention some points to others\"\n- **2.5** = (intermediate)\n- **2** = \"Potentially Interesting\" (doesn't resonate with reviewer)\n- **1.5** = (intermediate)\n- **1** = \"Not Exciting\" (not related to NLP)\n\n## (4) Confidence\n- **5** = \"Positive that my evaluation is correct\" with careful review\n- **4** = \"Quite sure\" but might miss something\n- **3** = \"Pretty sure\" but didn't check all details carefully\n- **2** = \"Willing to defend\" but likely missed details\n- **1** = \"Not my area\" or incomprehensible\n\n## (5) Reproducibility\n- **5** = \"They could easily reproduce the results\"\n- **4** = \"Mostly reproduce\" with minor variations possible\n- **3** = \"Reproduce with some difficulty\" (underspecified parameters)\n- **2** = \"Hard pressed to reproduce\" (unavailable data/insufficient details)\n- **1** = \"Would not be able to reproduce\" despite effort"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8860f592-6591-47fa-b978-f5709a359b4d","uuid":"004a0bca-c819-4c76-bfbb-c3e81e46cec1","timestamp":"2026-05-15T10:06:01.424Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":25365,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# ACL Rolling Review Scoring Scales\n\n## (1) Overall Assessment/Recommendation\n- **5** = \"Consider for Award\" (top 2.5% papers)\n- **4.5** = Borderline Award\n- **4** = Conference acceptance\n- **3.5** = Borderline Conference\n- **3** = Findings acceptance\n- **2.5** = Borderline Findings\n- **2** = Resubmit next cycle\n- **1.5** = Resubmit after next cycle\n- **1** = Do not resubmit\n\n## (2) Soundness\n- **5** = \"Excellent: This study is one of the most thorough I have seen\"\n- **4.5** = (intermediate)\n- **4** = \"Strong: This study provides sufficient support for all claims\"\n- **3.5** = (intermediate)\n- **3** = \"Acceptable: sufficient support for main claims\"\n- **2.5** = (intermediate)\n- **2** = \"Poor: main claims not sufficiently supported\"\n- **1.5** = (intermediate)\n- **1** = \"Major Issues: not sufficiently thorough to warrant publication\"\n\n## (3) Excitement/Novelty\n- **5** = \"Highly Exciting: I would recommend this paper to others\"\n- **4.5** = (intermediate)\n- **4** = \"Exciting: I would mention this paper to others\"\n- **3.5** = (intermediate)\n- **3** = \"Interesting: might mention some points to others\"\n- **2.5** = (intermediate)\n- **2** = \"Potentially Interesting\" (doesn't resonate with reviewer)\n- **1.5** = (intermediate)\n- **1** = \"Not Exciting\" (not related to NLP)\n\n## (4) Confidence\n- **5** = \"Positive that my evaluation is correct\" with careful review\n- **4** = \"Quite sure\" but might miss something\n- **3** = \"Pretty sure\" but didn't check all details carefully\n- **2** = \"Willing to defend\" but likely missed details\n- **1** = \"Not my area\" or incomprehensible\n\n## (5) Reproducibility\n- **5** = \"They could easily reproduce the results\"\n- **4** = \"Mostly reproduce\" with minor variations possible\n- **3** = \"Reproduce with some difficulty\" (underspecified parameters)\n- **2** = \"Hard pressed to reproduce\" (unavailable data/insufficient details)\n- **1** = \"Would not be able to reproduce\" despite effort","durationMs":5840,"url":"https://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01579nrdCvcWneUt8bNEAN5x","type":"tool_result","content":"# ARR Reviewer Guidelines: Evaluation Dimensions and Assessment\n\n## Key Evaluation Dimensions\n\nARR reviewers assess papers across three primary dimensions:\n\n1. **Soundness**: Technical correctness and validity. Per guidelines, \"soundness scores must be justified by the text of the review.\"\n\n2. **Excitement**: Personal interest level, described as \"orthogonal to the assessment of the soundness of the paper\" and reflecting subjective taste.\n\n3. **Overall Recommendation**: A composite score reflecting soundness, excitement, novelty, and potential impact for venue placement.\n\n## Soundness Assessment Guidance\n\nReviewers should verify that low soundness scores have explicit justification in the review text. The guidelines note that \"if you give a low Soundness score without finding any major faults, this means that your review is not a faithful explanation of your recommendation.\"\n\nKey soundness checks include:\n- Technical validity of methodology\n- Completeness of proofs and assumptions\n- Appropriate statistical analysis\n- Reproducibility details\n\n## Findings vs. Main Conference Standards\n\nThe guidelines specify that \"all papers recommended for Findings and main conferences are expected to be sufficiently sound and reproducible.\" However, main conference papers may receive acceptance based on impact even without personal excitement, such as \"improvements in efficiency\" or \"high-quality resource[s].\"\n\n## Review Structure\n\nReviews should follow this workflow:\n\n- **Skim reading first**: Assess research questions and scope before examining results\n- **In-depth reading**: Evaluate technical soundness and evidence\n- **Specificity requirement**: Provide concrete examples rather than vague critiques\n- **Score alignment check**: Ensure recommendations match detailed justifications\n- **Tone verification**: Maintain professional, constructive language"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8860f592-6591-47fa-b978-f5709a359b4d","uuid":"29d564ca-519a-4ee8-acd3-21396b418256","timestamp":"2026-05-15T10:06:03.702Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":100603,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# ARR Reviewer Guidelines: Evaluation Dimensions and Assessment\n\n## Key Evaluation Dimensions\n\nARR reviewers assess papers across three primary dimensions:\n\n1. **Soundness**: Technical correctness and validity. Per guidelines, \"soundness scores must be justified by the text of the review.\"\n\n2. **Excitement**: Personal interest level, described as \"orthogonal to the assessment of the soundness of the paper\" and reflecting subjective taste.\n\n3. **Overall Recommendation**: A composite score reflecting soundness, excitement, novelty, and potential impact for venue placement.\n\n## Soundness Assessment Guidance\n\nReviewers should verify that low soundness scores have explicit justification in the review text. The guidelines note that \"if you give a low Soundness score without finding any major faults, this means that your review is not a faithful explanation of your recommendation.\"\n\nKey soundness checks include:\n- Technical validity of methodology\n- Completeness of proofs and assumptions\n- Appropriate statistical analysis\n- Reproducibility details\n\n## Findings vs. Main Conference Standards\n\nThe guidelines specify that \"all papers recommended for Findings and main conferences are expected to be sufficiently sound and reproducible.\" However, main conference papers may receive acceptance based on impact even without personal excitement, such as \"improvements in efficiency\" or \"high-quality resource[s].\"\n\n## Review Structure\n\nReviews should follow this workflow:\n\n- **Skim reading first**: Assess research questions and scope before examining results\n- **In-depth reading**: Evaluate technical soundness and evidence\n- **Specificity requirement**: Provide concrete examples rather than vague critiques\n- **Score alignment check**: Ensure recommendations match detailed justifications\n- **Tone verification**: Maintain professional, constructive language","durationMs":5775,"url":"https://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines"}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01XqkXrZpZYDPEzRVbNxA1ED","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have all the information I need. Let me summarize what I know:\n\n**ARR/EMNLP Scoring Scale:**\n- Overall: 1-5 (5=Award, 4=Conference, 3=Findings, 2=Resubmit, 1=Do not resubmit)\n- Soundness: 1-5\n- Excitement: 1-5\n- Confidence: 1-5\n- Reproducibility: 1-5\n\n**Paper Summary:**\n\"The Constrained Decoding Bottleneck: Why Confidence Signals Predict but Cannot Select in Information Extraction\"\n\n\nThe work tests five different confidence signals for selecting the best output from multiple candidates in structured information extraction tasks using fine-tuned LLMs. While these signals can effectively predict output quality with reasonable accuracy, they fail when applied to constrained decoding—the core issue being that constrained decoding severely compresses the variance in log-probabilities, making it impossible to differentiate between candidates. The authors show this compression can be reversed by allowing free-form generation instead, which restores the signal's discriminative power.\n\nInterestingly, the ranking of which signals work best completely flips between constrained and free-form settings, with log-probability shifting from the worst performer to the best. Even a supervised verifier model trained specifically for this task underperforms the simpler free-form log-probability approach. The paper also validates these findings across multiple model architectures and datasets, and includes ablations demonstrating the critical role of decoding strategy in enabling effective confidence-based selection.\n\nNow I'm organizing the review process by splitting responsibilities between two reviewers—one focusing on methodology and experimental rigor, the other on novelty and positioning relative to existing work—then running them through independent assessment, cross-validation, and synthesis phases.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1469,"cache_read_input_tokens":51174,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1469},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8860f592-6591-47fa-b978-f5709a359b4d","uuid":"9649f37b-71d5-4bb1-8d78-304ccacf4243"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01XqkXrZpZYDPEzRVbNxA1ED","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have the paper, review context, and EMNLP/ARR scoring rubric. Dispatching two independent reviewers in parallel: R1 (Methods + Experiments) and R2 (Novelty + Related Work)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1469,"cache_read_input_tokens":51174,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1469},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8860f592-6591-47fa-b978-f5709a359b4d","uuid":"a7e2c3ce-37d5-4c0b-851e-bb8634643088"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01XqkXrZpZYDPEzRVbNxA1ED","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KV3rBMTrnDPFpq5YvDX9vP","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an ACL Rolling Review (ARR) submission targeting EMNLP 2026 Findings. Your focus: **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## ARR Scoring Scale\n- Overall: 5=Award, 4.5=Borderline Award, 4=Conference, 3.5=Borderline Conference, 3=Findings, 2.5=Borderline Findings, 2=Resubmit, 1.5=Resubmit after next cycle, 1=Do not resubmit\n- Soundness: 5=Excellent, 4=Strong, 3=Acceptable, 2=Poor, 1=Major Issues (half-point increments)\n- Excitement: 5=Highly Exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Interesting, 2=Potentially Interesting, 1=Not Exciting\n- Reproducibility: 5=Easily reproduce, 4=Mostly reproduce, 3=Some difficulty, 2=Hard pressed, 1=Cannot reproduce\n\n## Paper: \"The Constrained Decoding Bottleneck: Why Confidence Signals Predict but Cannot Select in Information Extraction\"\n\n### Abstract\nTest-time scaling via multi-sample generation improves LLM performance on reasoning tasks, but its effectiveness for structured information extraction (IE) remains unclear. The paper evaluates five confidence signals—log-probability, exact match, set similarity, token voting, and n-gram overlap—for selecting the best output among multiple candidates generated by fine-tuned LLMs on NER and RE. Key finding: a **regime-specific selection bottleneck** where signals achieve strong quality estimation (AUROC up to 0.75) but fail at best-of-N selection under grammar-constrained generation (ΔF1 = +0.6pp, p = 0.39). Constrained decoding compresses log-prob variance—60% of instances have within-sample range < 0.05. A controlled ablation (free-form generation) recovers variance by 10× and enables +5.0pp NER selection gain (p=0.008). Signal ranking reverses: log-prob goes from worst (ρ=0.21) to best (ρ=0.42). A supervised DeBERTa verifier achieves only +3.6pp under constrained decoding, below the zero-cost free-form log-prob gain, confirming the bottleneck is in decoding regime, not scoring function.\n\n### Method (Section 3)\n- Problem setting: Given input x, fine-tuned LLM generates N=8 candidates at T=1.0. Quality q = micro-F1 via type-grouped Hungarian matching.\n- Five signals: (1) LogProb—normalized sequence log-probability (only per-sample signal), (2) Set Jaccard (SJ)—avg pairwise Jaccard on entity/relation sets, (3) Fleiss' Kappa (FK)—inter-rater agreement treating samples as annotators, (4) Exact Match (EM)—fraction of identical output pairs, (5) Voting Confidence (VotConf)—entity-level voting fraction.\n- Critical distinction: SJ, FK, EM, VotConf produce instance-level scores (single score per instance x, cannot rank candidates). Only LogProb is per-sample. For selection with instance-level signals, they use consensus-proximity (select candidate closest to consensus, analogous to MBR).\n- Metrics: Spearman's ρ, AUROC (binary F1>0 vs F1=0), ECE, and selection F1 (Δpp vs greedy + bootstrap CI + permutation p-values).\n\n### Experimental Setup (Section 4)\n- Models: Qwen3-8B and LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct, fine-tuned on respective training sets\n- Datasets: SciERC (NER: 529, RE: 397) and CoNLL-2003 (NER: 3,453)\n- Decoding: Constrained via Outlines (JSON schema), Free-form (identical settings minus grammar)\n- Baselines: Greedy (T=0), random selection, oracle\n\n### Results (Section 5)\n**5.1 Quality Estimation (Table 1)**: On SciERC constrained, VotConf best ρ=0.379, LogProb worst ρ=0.205. EM leads AUROC=0.749 (NER). LogProb ECE=0.706 (worst calibration).\n\n**5.2 Correlation-Selection Gap (Table 3)**: No signal significantly beats greedy. LogProb best at +0.6pp but p=0.39, d=0.038. Oracle headroom is large (NER +13.2pp, RE +18.1pp).\n\n**Cross-model validation (Table 2)**: LLaMA×CoNLL vs Qwen×CoNLL: SJ ρ Δ=0.005. Both show structural > logprob pattern.\n\n**5.3 Log-Prob Compression**: Median within-sample range = 0.031, 60% instances range < 0.05.\n\n**5.4 Free-Form Ablation (Table 4)**: Variance recovers 10.3× (0.031→0.320). LogProb ρ: 0.205→0.420. Selection: +5.0pp NER (p=0.008, d=0.31, 95% CI [+1.8, +8.2]), +3.2pp RE (p=0.032).\n\n**Signal ranking reversal (Table 5)**: Constrained: LogProb(0.205) << SJ(0.360) << VotConf(0.379). Free-form: LogProb(0.420) > VotConf(0.390) > SJ(0.370).\n\n**5.5 Supervised Verifier (Tables 6-7)**: DeBERTa-v3-large, 5-fold CV. ρ=0.52, AUROC=0.82 (NER). Selection: +3.6pp (27.3% headroom). Free-form logprob (+5.0pp, 33.8%) > verifier (+3.6pp, 27.3%).\n\n**Appendix A: OOD (Table 8)**: SciERC→WNUT-17. Greedy F1 drops to 0.32. LogProb ρ=−0.08 (negative). Pattern persists.\n\n### Expected Experiment Coverage (from review context)\nThe review context lists 4 imagined experiments:\n1. exp-011 (OOD robustness) → Appendix A ✓\n2. exp-017 (LLaMA×CoNLL cross-model) → Table 2 ✓  \n3. exp-018 (free-form ablation, BREAKTHROUGH) → Section 5.4 ✓\n4. exp-019 (supervised verifier) → Section 5.5 ✓\nAll 4 experiments are covered.\n\n### Limitations stated by authors\n(1) Only 8B models, (2) Two NER + one RE dataset, (3) 90% parse rate may be lower for less fine-tuned models, (4) Only T=1.0 studied.\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a detailed R1 review focused on **methods and experiments**. Evaluate:\n\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the experimental design rigorous? Are the statistical tests appropriate? Is the causal claim (constrained decoding → compression → selection failure) well-supported?\n\n2. **Experimental completeness**: Are there missing baselines or ablations? Is the 2×2 model-dataset grid sufficient? Is N=8 enough?\n\n3. **Signal framework**: Is the instance-level vs per-sample distinction handled correctly? Is the consensus-proximity strategy for instance-level signals fair?\n\n4. **Free-form ablation validity**: Is this a fair controlled experiment? Are there confounds (e.g., parse failure selection bias)?\n\n5. **Verifier baseline fairness**: Is DeBERTa-v3-large a reasonable choice? Is 5-fold CV on 529 instances sufficient?\n\n6. **Statistical rigor**: Bootstrap CIs, permutation tests, effect sizes—adequate?\n\n7. **Reproducibility**: Sufficient detail to replicate?\n\n8. **Weaknesses and suggestions**: What are the key methodological weaknesses? What would strengthen the paper?\n\nFormat your review as:\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n(etc.)\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n(etc.)\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: ...\n(etc.)\n\n### Minor Issues\n- ...\n\n### Preliminary Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Overall: X/5\n- Confidence: X/5\n```\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Identify real methodological concerns, not nitpicks.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1469,"cache_read_input_tokens":51174,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1469},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8860f592-6591-47fa-b978-f5709a359b4d","uuid":"391cb5a2-ed1b-4e23-87aa-ba0654eff4f7"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a1f538a482e4a7730","tool_use_id":"toolu_01KV3rBMTrnDPFpq5YvDX9vP","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an ACL Rolling Review (ARR) submission targeting EMNLP 2026 Findings. Your focus: **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## ARR Scoring Scale\n- Overall: 5=Award, 4.5=Borderline Award, 4=Conference, 3.5=Borderline Conference, 3=Findings, 2.5=Borderline Findings, 2=Resubmit, 1.5=Resubmit after next cycle, 1=Do not resubmit\n- Soundness: 5=Excellent, 4=Strong, 3=Acceptable, 2=Poor, 1=Major Issues (half-point increments)\n- Excitement: 5=Highly Exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Interesting, 2=Potentially Interesting, 1=Not Exciting\n- Reproducibility: 5=Easily reproduce, 4=Mostly reproduce, 3=Some difficulty, 2=Hard pressed, 1=Cannot reproduce\n\n## Paper: \"The Constrained Decoding Bottleneck: Why Confidence Signals Predict but Cannot Select in Information Extraction\"\n\n### Abstract\nTest-time scaling via multi-sample generation improves LLM performance on reasoning tasks, but its effectiveness for structured information extraction (IE) remains unclear. The paper evaluates five confidence signals—log-probability, exact match, set similarity, token voting, and n-gram overlap—for selecting the best output among multiple candidates generated by fine-tuned LLMs on NER and RE. Key finding: a **regime-specific selection bottleneck** where signals achieve strong quality estimation (AUROC up to 0.75) but fail at best-of-N selection under grammar-constrained generation (ΔF1 = +0.6pp, p = 0.39). Constrained decoding compresses log-prob variance—60% of instances have within-sample range < 0.05. A controlled ablation (free-form generation) recovers variance by 10× and enables +5.0pp NER selection gain (p=0.008). Signal ranking reverses: log-prob goes from worst (ρ=0.21) to best (ρ=0.42). A supervised DeBERTa verifier achieves only +3.6pp under constrained decoding, below the zero-cost free-form log-prob gain, confirming the bottleneck is in decoding regime, not scoring function.\n\n### Method (Section 3)\n- Problem setting: Given input x, fine-tuned LLM generates N=8 candidates at T=1.0. Quality q = micro-F1 via type-grouped Hungarian matching.\n- Five signals: (1) LogProb—normalized sequence log-probability (only per-sample signal), (2) Set Jaccard (SJ)—avg pairwise Jaccard on entity/relation sets, (3) Fleiss' Kappa (FK)—inter-rater agreement treating samples as annotators, (4) Exact Match (EM)—fraction of identical output pairs, (5) Voting Confidence (VotConf)—entity-level voting fraction.\n- Critical distinction: SJ, FK, EM, VotConf produce instance-level scores (single score per instance x, cannot rank candidates). Only LogProb is per-sample. For selection with instance-level signals, they use consensus-proximity (select candidate closest to consensus, analogous to MBR).\n- Metrics: Spearman's ρ, AUROC (binary F1>0 vs F1=0), ECE, and selection F1 (Δpp vs greedy + bootstrap CI + permutation p-values).\n\n### Experimental Setup (Section 4)\n- Models: Qwen3-8B and LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct, fine-tuned on respective training sets\n- Datasets: SciERC (NER: 529, RE: 397) and CoNLL-2003 (NER: 3,453)\n- Decoding: Constrained via Outlines (JSON schema), Free-form (identical settings minus grammar)\n- Baselines: Greedy (T=0), random selection, oracle\n\n### Results (Section 5)\n**5.1 Quality Estimation (Table 1)**: On SciERC constrained, VotConf best ρ=0.379, LogProb worst ρ=0.205. EM leads AUROC=0.749 (NER). LogProb ECE=0.706 (worst calibration).\n\n**5.2 Correlation-Selection Gap (Table 3)**: No signal significantly beats greedy. LogProb best at +0.6pp but p=0.39, d=0.038. Oracle headroom is large (NER +13.2pp, RE +18.1pp).\n\n**Cross-model validation (Table 2)**: LLaMA×CoNLL vs Qwen×CoNLL: SJ ρ Δ=0.005. Both show structural > logprob pattern.\n\n**5.3 Log-Prob Compression**: Median within-sample range = 0.031, 60% instances range < 0.05.\n\n**5.4 Free-Form Ablation (Table 4)**: Variance recovers 10.3× (0.031→0.320). LogProb ρ: 0.205→0.420. Selection: +5.0pp NER (p=0.008, d=0.31, 95% CI [+1.8, +8.2]), +3.2pp RE (p=0.032).\n\n**Signal ranking reversal (Table 5)**: Constrained: LogProb(0.205) << SJ(0.360) << VotConf(0.379). Free-form: LogProb(0.420) > VotConf(0.390) > SJ(0.370).\n\n**5.5 Supervised Verifier (Tables 6-7)**: DeBERTa-v3-large, 5-fold CV. ρ=0.52, AUROC=0.82 (NER). Selection: +3.6pp (27.3% headroom). Free-form logprob (+5.0pp, 33.8%) > verifier (+3.6pp, 27.3%).\n\n**Appendix A: OOD (Table 8)**: SciERC→WNUT-17. Greedy F1 drops to 0.32. LogProb ρ=−0.08 (negative). Pattern persists.\n\n### Expected Experiment Coverage (from review context)\nThe review context lists 4 imagined experiments:\n1. exp-011 (OOD robustness) → Appendix A ✓\n2. exp-017 (LLaMA×CoNLL cross-model) → Table 2 ✓  \n3. exp-018 (free-form ablation, BREAKTHROUGH) → Section 5.4 ✓\n4. exp-019 (supervised verifier) → Section 5.5 ✓\nAll 4 experiments are covered.\n\n### Limitations stated by authors\n(1) Only 8B models, (2) Two NER + one RE dataset, (3) 90% parse rate may be lower for less fine-tuned models, (4) Only T=1.0 studied.\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a detailed R1 review focused on **methods and experiments**. Evaluate:\n\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the experimental design rigorous? Are the statistical tests appropriate? Is the causal claim (constrained decoding → compression → selection failure) well-supported?\n\n2. **Experimental completeness**: Are there missing baselines or ablations? Is the 2×2 model-dataset grid sufficient? Is N=8 enough?\n\n3. **Signal framework**: Is the instance-level vs per-sample distinction handled correctly? Is the consensus-proximity strategy for instance-level signals fair?\n\n4. **Free-form ablation validity**: Is this a fair controlled experiment? Are there confounds (e.g., parse failure selection bias)?\n\n5. **Verifier baseline fairness**: Is DeBERTa-v3-large a reasonable choice? Is 5-fold CV on 529 instances sufficient?\n\n6. **Statistical rigor**: Bootstrap CIs, permutation tests, effect sizes—adequate?\n\n7. **Reproducibility**: Sufficient detail to replicate?\n\n8. **Weaknesses and suggestions**: What are the key methodological weaknesses? What would strengthen the paper?\n\nFormat your review as:\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n(etc.)\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n(etc.)\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: ...\n(etc.)\n\n### Minor Issues\n- ...\n\n### Preliminary Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Overall: X/5\n- Confidence: X/5\n```\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Identify real methodological concerns, not nitpicks.\n","uuid":"c5a916bf-d8bd-4eef-9256-d22981f93f33","session_id":"8860f592-6591-47fa-b978-f5709a359b4d"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an ACL Rolling Review (ARR) submission targeting EMNLP 2026 Findings. Your focus: **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## ARR Scoring Scale\n- Overall: 5=Award, 4.5=Borderline Award, 4=Conference, 3.5=Borderline Conference, 3=Findings, 2.5=Borderline Findings, 2=Resubmit, 1.5=Resubmit after next cycle, 1=Do not resubmit\n- Soundness: 5=Excellent, 4=Strong, 3=Acceptable, 2=Poor, 1=Major Issues (half-point increments)\n- Excitement: 5=Highly Exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Interesting, 2=Potentially Interesting, 1=Not Exciting\n- Reproducibility: 5=Easily reproduce, 4=Mostly reproduce, 3=Some difficulty, 2=Hard pressed, 1=Cannot reproduce\n\n## Paper: \"The Constrained Decoding Bottleneck: Why Confidence Signals Predict but Cannot Select in Information Extraction\"\n\n### Abstract\nTest-time scaling via multi-sample generation improves LLM performance on reasoning tasks, but its effectiveness for structured information extraction (IE) remains unclear. The paper evaluates five confidence signals—log-probability, exact match, set similarity, token voting, and n-gram overlap—for selecting the best output among multiple candidates generated by fine-tuned LLMs on NER and RE. Key finding: a **regime-specific selection bottleneck** where signals achieve strong quality estimation (AUROC up to 0.75) but fail at best-of-N selection under grammar-constrained generation (ΔF1 = +0.6pp, p = 0.39). Constrained decoding compresses log-prob variance—60% of instances have within-sample range < 0.05. A controlled ablation (free-form generation) recovers variance by 10× and enables +5.0pp NER selection gain (p=0.008). Signal ranking reverses: log-prob goes from worst (ρ=0.21) to best (ρ=0.42). A supervised DeBERTa verifier achieves only +3.6pp under constrained decoding, below the zero-cost free-form log-prob gain, confirming the bottleneck is in decoding regime, not scoring function.\n\n### Method (Section 3)\n- Problem setting: Given input x, fine-tuned LLM generates N=8 candidates at T=1.0. Quality q = micro-F1 via type-grouped Hungarian matching.\n- Five signals: (1) LogProb—normalized sequence log-probability (only per-sample signal), (2) Set Jaccard (SJ)—avg pairwise Jaccard on entity/relation sets, (3) Fleiss' Kappa (FK)—inter-rater agreement treating samples as annotators, (4) Exact Match (EM)—fraction of identical output pairs, (5) Voting Confidence (VotConf)—entity-level voting fraction.\n- Critical distinction: SJ, FK, EM, VotConf produce instance-level scores (single score per instance x, cannot rank candidates). Only LogProb is per-sample. For selection with instance-level signals, they use consensus-proximity (select candidate closest to consensus, analogous to MBR).\n- Metrics: Spearman's ρ, AUROC (binary F1>0 vs F1=0), ECE, and selection F1 (Δpp vs greedy + bootstrap CI + permutation p-values).\n\n### Experimental Setup (Section 4)\n- Models: Qwen3-8B and LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct, fine-tuned on respective training sets\n- Datasets: SciERC (NER: 529, RE: 397) and CoNLL-2003 (NER: 3,453)\n- Decoding: Constrained via Outlines (JSON schema), Free-form (identical settings minus grammar)\n- Baselines: Greedy (T=0), random selection, oracle\n\n### Results (Section 5)\n**5.1 Quality Estimation (Table 1)**: On SciERC constrained, VotConf best ρ=0.379, LogProb worst ρ=0.205. EM leads AUROC=0.749 (NER). LogProb ECE=0.706 (worst calibration).\n\n**5.2 Correlation-Selection Gap (Table 3)**: No signal significantly beats greedy. LogProb best at +0.6pp but p=0.39, d=0.038. Oracle headroom is large (NER +13.2pp, RE +18.1pp).\n\n**Cross-model validation (Table 2)**: LLaMA×CoNLL vs Qwen×CoNLL: SJ ρ Δ=0.005. Both show structural > logprob pattern.\n\n**5.3 Log-Prob Compression**: Median within-sample range = 0.031, 60% instances range < 0.05.\n\n**5.4 Free-Form Ablation (Table 4)**: Variance recovers 10.3× (0.031→0.320). LogProb ρ: 0.205→0.420. Selection: +5.0pp NER (p=0.008, d=0.31, 95% CI [+1.8, +8.2]), +3.2pp RE (p=0.032).\n\n**Signal ranking reversal (Table 5)**: Constrained: LogProb(0.205) << SJ(0.360) << VotConf(0.379). Free-form: LogProb(0.420) > VotConf(0.390) > SJ(0.370).\n\n**5.5 Supervised Verifier (Tables 6-7)**: DeBERTa-v3-large, 5-fold CV. ρ=0.52, AUROC=0.82 (NER). Selection: +3.6pp (27.3% headroom). Free-form logprob (+5.0pp, 33.8%) > verifier (+3.6pp, 27.3%).\n\n**Appendix A: OOD (Table 8)**: SciERC→WNUT-17. Greedy F1 drops to 0.32. LogProb ρ=−0.08 (negative). Pattern persists.\n\n### Expected Experiment Coverage (from review context)\nThe review context lists 4 imagined experiments:\n1. exp-011 (OOD robustness) → Appendix A ✓\n2. exp-017 (LLaMA×CoNLL cross-model) → Table 2 ✓  \n3. exp-018 (free-form ablation, BREAKTHROUGH) → Section 5.4 ✓\n4. exp-019 (supervised verifier) → Section 5.5 ✓\nAll 4 experiments are covered.\n\n### Limitations stated by authors\n(1) Only 8B models, (2) Two NER + one RE dataset, (3) 90% parse rate may be lower for less fine-tuned models, (4) Only T=1.0 studied.\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a detailed R1 review focused on **methods and experiments**. Evaluate:\n\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the experimental design rigorous? Are the statistical tests appropriate? Is the causal claim (constrained decoding → compression → selection failure) well-supported?\n\n2. **Experimental completeness**: Are there missing baselines or ablations? Is the 2×2 model-dataset grid sufficient? Is N=8 enough?\n\n3. **Signal framework**: Is the instance-level vs per-sample distinction handled correctly? Is the consensus-proximity strategy for instance-level signals fair?\n\n4. **Free-form ablation validity**: Is this a fair controlled experiment? Are there confounds (e.g., parse failure selection bias)?\n\n5. **Verifier baseline fairness**: Is DeBERTa-v3-large a reasonable choice? Is 5-fold CV on 529 instances sufficient?\n\n6. **Statistical rigor**: Bootstrap CIs, permutation tests, effect sizes—adequate?\n\n7. **Reproducibility**: Sufficient detail to replicate?\n\n8. **Weaknesses and suggestions**: What are the key methodological weaknesses? What would strengthen the paper?\n\nFormat your review as:\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n(etc.)\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n(etc.)\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: ...\n(etc.)\n\n### Minor Issues\n- ...\n\n### Preliminary Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Overall: X/5\n- Confidence: X/5\n```\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Identify real methodological concerns, not nitpicks.\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01KV3rBMTrnDPFpq5YvDX9vP","session_id":"8860f592-6591-47fa-b978-f5709a359b4d","uuid":"9f64c3c9-0e6c-4e1b-bff8-2f42f01533c7","timestamp":"2026-05-15T10:07:10.324Z"}
