{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019eEbT7wWAd7KowXjGEqYgT","type":"tool_result","content":"docs/paper/main.bbl:29:  Plappert, Tworek, Hilton, Nakano, Hesse, and Schulman}]{cobbe2021verifiers}\ndocs/paper/main.bbl:33:\\newblock \\href {https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.14168} {Training verifiers to solve\ndocs/paper/conclusion.tex:8:A direct free-form generation ablation would confirm whether the bottleneck is decoding-regime-specific.\ndocs/paper/conclusion.tex:9:Trained verifiers, relaxed decoding constraints, scaling to 70B+ models, and conformal prediction are promising directions for closing the gap.\ndocs/paper/discussion.tex:24:Our diagnosis predicts that LP score compression should diminish under free-form (unconstrained) generation, where the full token vocabulary is available at each step.\ndocs/paper/discussion.tex:25:A controlled ablation---comparing LP selection under constrained versus free-form decoding on identical instances, with post-hoc JSON parsing for the free-form condition---would directly test whether the bottleneck is decoding-regime-specific.\ndocs/paper/discussion.tex:26:\\textit{[Planned experiment: free-form ablation on SciERC NER/RE.]}\ndocs/paper/discussion.tex:27:All findings reported in this paper are specific to the constrained decoding regime; whether the gap persists under relaxed or free-form decoding remains an open question.\ndocs/paper/discussion.tex:31:Effective selection likely requires either per-sample signals such as trained verifiers \\cite{cobbe2021verifiers, venktesh2025trust} or relaxation of the constrained-decoding regime that compresses LP resolution; $N$-scaling analysis (Appendix~\\ref{app:additional}) shows $N{=}8$ captures most quality estimation benefit.\ndocs/paper/sections/related_work.tex:5:Extensions to free-form text use LLM-based comparison \\cite{chen2024usc}, atomic sub-claim decomposition \\cite{thirukovalluru2024atomic}, or cross-sample hallucination detection \\cite{manakul2023selfcheckgpt}.\ndocs/paper/sections/related_work.tex:25:Conformal prediction \\cite{fisch2021conformal, angelopoulos2021conformal} provides coverage guarantees; trained verifiers \\cite{cobbe2021verifiers, lightman2024verify, venktesh2025trust} and reward models \\cite{wang2024mathshepherd} learn per-sample scoring.\ndocs/paper/sections/experiments.tex:133:This gap has direct consequences for test-time scaling in IE \\cite{brown2024monkeys, snell2024scaling}: closing it likely requires per-sample signals such as trained verifiers \\cite{cobbe2021verifiers}.\ndocs/paper/sections/conclusion.tex:8:A direct free-form generation ablation would confirm whether the bottleneck is decoding-regime-specific.\ndocs/paper/sections/conclusion.tex:9:Trained verifiers, relaxed decoding constraints, scaling to 70B+ models, and conformal prediction are promising directions for closing the gap.\ndocs/paper/sections/discussion.tex:24:Our diagnosis predicts that LP score compression should diminish under free-form (unconstrained) generation, where the full token vocabulary is available at each step.\ndocs/paper/sections/discussion.tex:25:A controlled ablation---comparing LP selection under constrained versus free-form decoding on identical instances, with post-hoc JSON parsing for the free-form condition---would directly test whether the bottleneck is decoding-regime-specific.\ndocs/paper/sections/discussion.tex:26:\\textit{[Planned experiment: free-form ablation on SciERC NER/RE.]}\ndocs/paper/sections/discussion.tex:27:All findings reported in this paper are specific to the constrained decoding regime; whether the gap persists under relaxed or free-form decoding remains an open question.\ndocs/paper/sections/discussion.tex:31:Effective selection likely requires either per-sample signals such as trained verifiers \\cite{cobbe2021verifiers, venktesh2025trust} or relaxation of the constrained-decoding regime that compresses LP resolution; $N$-scaling analysis (Appendix~\\ref{app:additional}) shows $N{=}8$ captures most quality estimation benefit.\ndocs/paper/sections/limitations.tex:9:Our analysis of selection potential uses only unsupervised signals.\ndocs/paper/sections/limitations.tex:10:We do not train a dedicated verifier or reward model, leaving unknown how much of the oracle headroom a supervised approach could recover.\ndocs/paper/sections/limitations.tex:16:Constrained decoding (Outlines/vLLM) enforces schema compliance, potentially compressing per-sample diversity and contributing to the correlation-selection gap beyond what free-form generation would exhibit.\ndocs/paper/sections/limitations.tex:17:Our non-tied subset analysis (\\S\\ref{sec:discussion}) suggests this compression is the primary driver---quantifying the gap under free-form generation is an important open question.\ndocs/paper/sections/introduction.tex:26:Our findings are specific to the constrained decoding regime; whether the gap persists under free-form generation remains an open question.\ndocs/paper/references.bib:27:@article{cobbe2021verifiers,\ndocs/paper/references.bib:28:  title = {Training Verifiers to Solve Math Word Problems},\ndocs/paper/main.aux:244:\\citation{cobbe2021verifiers,lightman2024verify,venktesh2025trust}\ndocs/paper/main.aux:587:\\citation{cobbe2021verifiers}\ndocs/paper/main.aux:690:\\citation{cobbe2021verifiers,venktesh2025trust}\ndocs/paper/main.aux:866:\\bibcite{cobbe2021verifiers}{{4}{2021}{{Cobbe et~al.}}{{Cobbe, Kosaraju, Bavarian, Chen, Jun, Kaiser, Plappert, Tworek, Hilton, Nakano, Hesse, and Schulman}}}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01KV3rBMTrnDPFpq5YvDX9vP","session_id":"8860f592-6591-47fa-b978-f5709a359b4d","uuid":"7fdebe8d-b2df-4646-915a-a65f4692264c","timestamp":"2026-05-15T10:08:02.068Z"}
