{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01L9vCDjBAyRX5gjE7FnJwg4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01C5TcWotyPXDLo7KA2NMiDh","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_1/projects/struct_self_consist_ie/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T092545/best/paper.tex","limit":100},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2722,"cache_read_input_tokens":56935,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2722,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01GJcwQEcAFdZugRUsDbxRDD","session_id":"8860f592-6591-47fa-b978-f5709a359b4d","uuid":"0bfef0ef-180b-4701-bce0-07ae78c29998"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01C5TcWotyPXDLo7KA2NMiDh","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n4\t\\usepackage{times}\n5\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n6\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n7\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n8\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n9\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n10\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n11\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n12\t\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n13\t\\usepackage{caption}\n14\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n15\t\n16\t\\newcommand{\\rmark}[1]{\\textcolor{red}{#1}}\n17\t\\newcommand{\\bmark}[1]{\\textbf{#1}}\n18\t\n19\t\\title{The Constrained Decoding Bottleneck: Why Confidence Signals\\\\Predict but Cannot Select in Information Extraction}\n20\t\n21\t\\author{Anonymous}\n22\t\n23\t\\date{}\n24\t\n25\t\\begin{document}\n26\t\\maketitle\n27\t\n28\t% ============================================================\n29\t\\begin{abstract}\n30\tTest-time scaling via multi-sample generation improves LLM performance on reasoning tasks, but its effectiveness for structured information extraction (IE) remains unclear. We systematically evaluate five confidence signals---spanning log-probability, exact match, set similarity, token voting, and n-gram overlap---for selecting the best output among multiple candidates generated by fine-tuned LLMs on named entity recognition (NER) and relation extraction (RE). We identify a \\emph{regime-specific selection bottleneck}: while these signals reliably estimate output quality (AUROC up to 0.75), they fail to select better candidates than greedy decoding under grammar-constrained generation ($\\Delta$F1 $= +0.6$pp, $p = 0.39$). Diagnosis reveals that constrained decoding compresses log-probability variance---60\\% of instances have within-sample log-prob range $< 0.05$---destroying the only per-sample ranking signal. A controlled ablation confirms the causal mechanism: removing grammar constraints recovers log-prob variance by 10$\\times$ and enables significant selection gains (NER $+5.0$pp, $p = 0.008$). The signal ranking itself reverses: log-prob shifts from worst under constrained decoding ($\\rho = 0.21$) to best under free-form generation ($\\rho = 0.42$). A supervised DeBERTa verifier achieves only $+3.6$pp under constrained decoding, below the zero-cost free-form log-prob gain, confirming that the bottleneck lies in the decoding regime, not the scoring function. Our results provide the first controlled evidence that constrained decoding creates a fundamental selection barrier for structured IE, and suggest relaxed decoding with post-hoc validation as a practical mitigation path.\n31\t\\end{abstract}\n32\t\n33\t% ============================================================\n34\t\\section{Introduction}\n35\t\\label{sec:intro}\n36\t\n37\tMulti-sample generation at test time has emerged as a powerful strategy for improving LLM outputs. Self-consistency \\citep{wang2023selfconsistency} selects answers by majority vote, MBR decoding \\citep{eikema2022sampling} selects translations by consensus similarity, and best-of-N sampling with reward models \\citep{cobbe2021training,lightman2023lets} selects reasoning chains by learned quality scores. These methods share a common template: generate $N$ candidates, score each, select the best.\n38\t\n39\tFor structured information extraction---NER and RE tasks where outputs are \\emph{sets} of entities or relation triples---this template faces a challenge that prior work has not systematically examined. Modern IE systems increasingly use constrained (grammar-guided) decoding \\citep{willard2023efficient} to guarantee well-formed structured outputs, but the interaction between constrained decoding and candidate selection has not been studied. Meanwhile, the few studies on consistency-based confidence for IE \\citep{nedellec2026consistency} rely on surface-level string matching and do not examine selection performance.\n40\t\n41\tWe present a systematic evaluation of five confidence signals for structured IE quality estimation and selection, and discover a \\textbf{regime-specific selection bottleneck} caused by constrained decoding. Our investigation proceeds in three stages:\n42\t\n43\t\\textbf{(1) Quality estimation succeeds, selection fails.} We evaluate five signal families on SciERC and CoNLL-2003 across two model architectures (Qwen3-8B, LLaMA-3.1-8B). All structural signals achieve strong quality estimation (AUROC up to 0.75). Yet no signal significantly outperforms greedy decoding at best-of-$N$ selection (NER: $+0.6$pp, $p = 0.39$; RE: greedy dominates). We call this the \\emph{correlation--selection gap}.\n44\t\n45\t\\textbf{(2) Constrained decoding compresses log-probabilities.} We diagnose the mechanism: grammar-guided decoding masks invalid tokens at each step, collapsing the log-probability distribution. Across 529 NER instances, 60\\% have within-sample log-prob range below 0.05---candidates are indistinguishable to the only per-sample ranking signal. The four structural signals (SJ, FK, EM, voting) are instance-level aggregates that cannot rank candidates by construction.\n46\t\n47\t\\textbf{(3) Controlled ablation confirms the causal mechanism.} Removing grammar constraints while keeping all other variables fixed (same model, data, $N$, temperature) recovers log-prob variance by 10$\\times$ and enables statistically significant selection: NER $+5.0$pp ($p = 0.008$), RE $+3.2$pp ($p = 0.032$). The signal ranking reverses entirely---log-prob goes from worst ($\\rho = 0.21$) to best ($\\rho = 0.42$), while structural signals remain stable.\n48\t\n49\tA supervised DeBERTa verifier trained with labeled data achieves only $+3.6$pp under constrained decoding, below the zero-cost free-form log-prob gain of $+5.0$pp. This confirms that the bottleneck is the decoding regime, not the quality of the scoring function.\n50\t\n51\tOur contributions are: (1) the first controlled identification and verification of constrained decoding as a causal bottleneck for test-time selection in structured IE; (2) a systematic five-signal evaluation framework with cross-model, cross-dataset validation; and (3) evidence that relaxed decoding is a more effective mitigation than stronger scoring functions.\n52\t\n53\t% ============================================================\n54\t\\section{Related Work}\n55\t\\label{sec:related}\n56\t\n57\t\\paragraph{Test-Time Scaling and Sample Selection.}\n58\tSelf-consistency \\citep{wang2023selfconsistency} selects answers by majority vote over chain-of-thought samples. Extensions include confidence-weighted voting \\citep[CISC;][]{cisc2025}, KL-divergence-based reward-free selection \\citep[Self-Certainty;][]{selfcertainty2026}, reliability-aware adaptive sampling \\citep[ReASC;][]{reasc2026}, ranked voting \\citep{rankedvoting2025}, and atomic sub-claim consistency \\citep{atomicsc2024}. These methods target discrete-answer reasoning tasks. We address set-structured IE outputs where majority vote is undefined and candidate comparison requires structural metrics.\n59\t\n60\t\\paragraph{Uncertainty Quantification for LLMs.}\n61\t\\citet{uqsurvey2025} and \\citet{uqcalibsurvey2025} provide comprehensive surveys. Semantic entropy \\citep{kuhn2023semantic} clusters generations by meaning; semantic entropy probes \\citep{seprobes2024} and latent self-consistency \\citep{latentsc2025} reduce cost via embedding spaces. SelfCheckGPT \\citep{manakul2023selfcheckgpt} detects hallucinations via sample consistency. CoCoA \\citep{cocoa2025} combines confidence and consistency for MBR-style selection on QA and summarization. \\citet{vashurin2025systematic} systematically evaluate four UQ methods but do not cover structured IE. Our work fills this gap by evaluating confidence signals specifically for structured outputs under constrained decoding.\n62\t\n63\t\\paragraph{Confidence and Calibration.}\n64\tCalibration of neural models \\citep{guo2017calibration}, selective prediction \\citep{geifman2017selective,abstentionsurvey2025}, and LLM confidence estimation \\citep{confestsurvey2024} are well-studied for classification and generation. For structured prediction, calibration methods exist for sequences \\citep{kuleshov2015calibration} and NER \\citep{jagannatha2022calibrating}. Token-level NER uncertainty has been studied via MC-dropout \\citep{gal2016dropout,xiao2019quantifying,shelmanov2021certain} and ensembles \\citep{lakshminarayanan2017simple,he2020towards}. We evaluate \\emph{sample-level} confidence for multi-candidate selection, a distinct problem from token-level uncertainty.\n65\t\n66\t\\paragraph{Consistency for Structured IE.}\n67\tMost closely related, \\citet{nedellec2026consistency} study consistency--accuracy correlation in hard-prompted LLMs for NER and RE. They use surface-level string matching, which they acknowledge ``overlooks semantically consistent outputs.'' Our Set Jaccard metric directly addresses this limitation. Unlike their work, we (a) study best-of-$N$ selection beyond correlation, (b) use fine-tuned models with constrained decoding, (c) evaluate five signal families, and (d) provide a controlled free-form ablation identifying the causal bottleneck.\n68\t\n69\t\\paragraph{Constrained Decoding and Structured Generation.}\n70\tGrammar-guided generation \\citep{willard2023efficient} guarantees syntactically valid outputs. While extensively used in IE pipelines, its effect on output diversity and downstream selection has not been studied. We provide the first evidence that grammar masking compresses log-probability variance and creates a selection bottleneck.\n71\t\n72\t\\paragraph{Verifiers and Reward Models.}\n73\tOutcome reward models \\citep{cobbe2021training} and process reward models \\citep{lightman2023lets} enable best-of-$N$ selection for reasoning tasks. Test-time compute scaling theory \\citep{snell2024scaling,brown2024large} analyzes when additional samples help. We complement this line by showing that in constrained-decoding IE, the bottleneck is candidate diversity rather than verifier quality.\n74\t\n75\t% ============================================================\n76\t\\section{Method}\n77\t\\label{sec:method}\n78\t\n79\t\\subsection{Problem Setting}\n80\t\\label{sec:problem}\n81\t\n82\tGiven an input $x$ (e.g., a sentence), a fine-tuned LLM generates $N$ candidate outputs $\\{y_1, \\ldots, y_N\\}$ via sampling at temperature $T$. Each $y_i$ is a structured object: a set of entities for NER or a set of relation triples for RE. The \\textbf{quality estimation} problem is to predict the quality $q(y_i, y^*)$ of each candidate relative to ground truth $y^*$ without access to $y^*$. The \\textbf{selection} problem is to choose $\\hat{y} = \\arg\\max_i s(y_i)$ using a scoring function $s$ to maximize $q(\\hat{y}, y^*)$.\n83\t\n84\tWe evaluate quality $q$ as micro-F1 between the candidate's entity/relation set and the gold set, using type-grouped Hungarian matching to handle set alignment.\n85\t\n86\t\\subsection{Confidence Signals}\n87\t\\label{sec:signals}\n88\t\n89\tWe evaluate five signals spanning four families:\n90\t\n91\t\\paragraph{Log-probability (LogProb).}\n92\tThe normalized sequence log-probability:\n93\t\\begin{equation}\n94\ts_{\\text{lp}}(y_i) = \\frac{1}{|y_i|} \\sum_{t=1}^{|y_i|} \\log p_\\theta(y_i^{(t)} | y_i^{(<t)}, x)\n95\t\\end{equation}\n96\tThis is the \\emph{only} signal that produces a per-sample score enabling direct candidate ranking.\n97\t\n98\t\\paragraph{Set Jaccard Similarity (SJ).}\n99\tThe average pairwise Jaccard similarity between entity/relation sets:\n100\t\\begin{equation}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01GJcwQEcAFdZugRUsDbxRDD","session_id":"8860f592-6591-47fa-b978-f5709a359b4d","uuid":"c47baba1-8d4a-40ec-b386-e07db65400e3","timestamp":"2026-05-15T10:08:29.642Z"}
