{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UrQ9Q1gPkhRrMiMXQ6tFYR","type":"tool_result","content":"100\t\\begin{equation}\n101\ts_{\\text{sj}}(x) = \\frac{1}{\\binom{N}{2}} \\sum_{i < j} \\frac{|S_i \\cap S_j|}{|S_i \\cup S_j|}\n102\t\\end{equation}\n103\twhere $S_i$ is the entity set extracted from $y_i$. SJ measures structural agreement at the set level, robust to surface-form variation.\n104\t\n105\t\\paragraph{Fleiss' Kappa (FK).}\n106\tInter-rater agreement treating the $N$ samples as annotators and entity spans as items. Measures consistency at the entity level.\n107\t\n108\t\\paragraph{Exact Match Rate (EM).}\n109\tThe fraction of sample pairs that produce identical output strings:\n110\t\\begin{equation}\n111\ts_{\\text{em}}(x) = \\frac{1}{\\binom{N}{2}} \\sum_{i < j} \\mathbb{1}[y_i = y_j]\n112\t\\end{equation}\n113\t\n114\t\\paragraph{Voting Confidence (VotConf).}\n115\tEntity-level voting: for each unique entity across $N$ samples, the fraction of samples that produce it. The instance score is the average voting confidence over all entities appearing in $\\geq N/2$ samples.\n116\t\n117\t\\paragraph{Instance-level vs.\\ per-sample distinction.}\n118\tA critical observation: SJ, FK, EM, and VotConf produce a \\emph{single score per instance} $x$---they measure agreement across the $N$ candidates but do not differentiate among them. Only LogProb produces a per-sample score $s(y_i)$. For best-of-$N$ \\emph{selection}, instance-level signals can filter instances (accept/abstain) but cannot rank candidates. This is a definitional property, not an empirical finding, yet it is commonly overlooked when repurposing quality estimation signals for selection.\n119\t\n120\tFor selection with instance-level signals, we use the consensus-proximity strategy: select the candidate closest to the consensus (highest average similarity to other candidates), analogous to MBR decoding \\citep{eikema2022sampling}.\n121\t\n122\t\\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}\n123\t\\label{sec:metrics}\n124\t\n125\t\\paragraph{Quality estimation.} Spearman's $\\rho$ (rank correlation between signal and F1 across instances), AUROC (binary classification: F1 $> 0$ vs.\\ F1 $= 0$), and Expected Calibration Error (ECE).\n126\t\n127\t\\paragraph{Selection.} Best-of-$N$ F1: the F1 of the selected candidate. We report $\\Delta$pp versus greedy decoding, with bootstrap confidence intervals (1000 iterations) and permutation $p$-values.\n128\t\n129\t% ============================================================\n130\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n131\t\\label{sec:setup}\n132\t\n133\t\\paragraph{Models.} Qwen3-8B \\citep{qwen2024} and LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct \\citep{llama31}, both fine-tuned on the respective training sets.\n134\t\n135\t\\paragraph{Datasets.} SciERC \\citep{luan2018multi} (NER: 529 test instances, RE: 397) and CoNLL-2003 \\citep{tjong2003conll} (NER: 3,453 test instances). SciERC provides both NER and RE evaluation; CoNLL-2003 provides a second high-resource NER benchmark.\n136\t\n137\t\\paragraph{Decoding.}\n138\t\\emph{Constrained}: Outlines grammar-guided decoding \\citep{willard2023efficient} with JSON schema constraints, $N = 8$ samples at $T = 1.0$.\n139\t\\emph{Free-form}: identical settings without grammar constraints; outputs parsed post-hoc (90\\% NER parse rate, 88\\% RE).\n140\t\n141\t\\paragraph{Baselines.} Greedy decoding ($T = 0$, single output), random selection from $N$ candidates, and oracle selection (best candidate by true F1).\n142\t\n143\t% ============================================================\n144\t\\section{Results}\n145\t\\label{sec:results}\n146\t\n147\t\\subsection{Quality Estimation Under Constrained Decoding}\n148\t\\label{sec:qe}\n149\t\n150\tTable~\\ref{tab:qe_scierc} presents quality estimation results on SciERC with Qwen3-8B under constrained decoding. All structural signals substantially outperform log-probability on ranking ($\\rho$), while log-probability shows the worst calibration (ECE $= 0.706$). On binary discrimination (AUROC), exact match leads for NER (0.749) and RE (0.706).\n151\t\n152\t\\begin{table}[t]\n153\t\\centering\n154\t\\caption{Quality estimation on SciERC (Qwen3-8B, constrained decoding, $N\\!=\\!8$, $T\\!=\\!1.0$). $\\rho$: Spearman correlation with F1. Bold: best per column.}\n155\t\\label{tab:qe_scierc}\n156\t\\small\n157\t\\begin{tabular}{l ccc ccc}\n158\t\\toprule\n159\t& \\multicolumn{3}{c}{\\textbf{NER} ($n = 529$)} & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{\\textbf{RE} ($n = 397$)} \\\\\n160\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \\cmidrule(lr){5-7}\n161\tSignal & $\\rho$ & AUROC & ECE & $\\rho$ & AUROC & ECE \\\\\n162\t\\midrule\n163\tVotConf & \\bmark{0.379} & 0.684 & 0.381 & --- & \\bmark{0.694} & --- \\\\\n164\tSJ      & 0.360 & 0.692 & 0.453 & --- & 0.670 & --- \\\\\n165\tEM      & 0.295 & \\bmark{0.749} & \\bmark{0.353} & --- & 0.706 & --- \\\\\n166\tFK      & 0.267 & 0.629 & 0.451 & --- & 0.609 & --- \\\\\n167\tLogProb & 0.205 & 0.578 & 0.706 & --- & 0.601 & --- \\\\\n168\t\\bottomrule\n169\t\\end{tabular}\n170\t\\end{table}\n171\t\n172\t\\paragraph{Cross-model validation.}\n173\tTable~\\ref{tab:crossmodel} shows that signal rankings are consistent across models. On CoNLL-2003, LLaMA-3.1-8B and Qwen3-8B produce near-identical SJ $\\rho$ values ($\\Delta = 0.005$), and both show the same structural-beats-logprob pattern ($\\Delta\\rho > 0.12$). This rules out model-specific artifacts and establishes the signal hierarchy as a task property.\n174\t\n175\t\\begin{table}[t]\n176\t\\centering\n177\t\\caption{Cross-model validation on CoNLL-2003 NER (constrained, $N\\!=\\!8$, $T\\!=\\!1.0$). Both models show identical signal ranking with structural signals dominating log-prob.}\n178\t\\label{tab:crossmodel}\n179\t\\small\n180\t\\begin{tabular}{l cc cc}\n181\t\\toprule\n182\t& \\multicolumn{2}{c}{\\textbf{LLaMA-3.1-8B}} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{\\textbf{Qwen3-8B}} \\\\\n183\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-3} \\cmidrule(lr){4-5}\n184\tSignal & $\\rho$ & AUROC & $\\rho$ & AUROC \\\\\n185\t\\midrule\n186\tSJ      & 0.431 & 0.704 & 0.436 & --- \\\\\n187\tEM      & 0.431 & 0.705 & --- & --- \\\\\n188\tFK      & 0.428 & 0.703 & --- & --- \\\\\n189\tVotConf & 0.426 & 0.709 & --- & --- \\\\\n190\tLogProb & 0.307 & 0.761 & 0.316 & --- \\\\\n191\t\\midrule\n192\t\\multicolumn{5}{l}{\\small Greedy F1: LLaMA 0.926, Oracle: 0.944, Headroom: 0.018} \\\\\n193\t\\bottomrule\n194\t\\end{tabular}\n195\t\\end{table}\n196\t\n197\t\\subsection{The Correlation--Selection Gap}\n198\t\\label{sec:gap}\n199\t\n200\tDespite strong quality estimation, no signal achieves significant selection gains under constrained decoding (Table~\\ref{tab:selection_constrained}). On NER, the best signal (LogProb) achieves $+0.6$pp over greedy, with bootstrap $p = 0.39$ and Cohen's $d = 0.038$ (negligible effect). On RE, greedy decoding outperforms all signals. The oracle ceiling reveals substantial headroom (NER: $+13.2$pp, RE: $+18.1$pp), confirming that better candidates \\emph{exist} among the $N$ samples but cannot be identified.\n201\t\n202\t\\begin{table}[t]\n203\t\\centering\n204\t\\caption{Best-of-$N$ selection F1 under constrained decoding (SciERC, Qwen3-8B). No signal significantly outperforms greedy. Oracle headroom confirms selection failure, not candidate quality.}\n205\t\\label{tab:selection_constrained}\n206\t\\small\n207\t\\begin{tabular}{l cc}\n208\t\\toprule\n209\tStrategy & NER F1 & RE F1 \\\\\n210\t\\midrule\n211\tRandom   & 0.608 & 0.354 \\\\\n212\tGreedy   & 0.644 & \\bmark{0.404} \\\\\n213\t\\midrule\n214\tVotConf (consensus-prox.)  & 0.634 & 0.359 \\\\\n215\tSJ (consensus-prox.)       & 0.645 & 0.386 \\\\\n216\tLogProb                    & \\bmark{0.650} & 0.395 \\\\\n217\tEnsemble (LR)              & 0.645 & 0.386 \\\\\n218\t\\midrule\n219\tOracle                     & 0.776 & 0.585 \\\\\n220\t\\midrule\n221\t\\multicolumn{3}{l}{\\small LogProb vs Greedy (NER): $\\Delta = +0.6$pp, $p = 0.39$, $d = 0.038$} \\\\\n222\t\\multicolumn{3}{l}{\\small Bootstrap 95\\% CI: [$-3.0$, $+4.2$]pp} \\\\\n223\t\\bottomrule\n224\t\\end{tabular}\n225\t\\end{table}\n226\t\n227\t\\subsection{Diagnosing the Bottleneck: Log-Prob Compression}\n228\t\\label{sec:diagnosis}\n229\t\n230\tWhy does log-probability---the only per-sample signal---fail at selection? Under constrained decoding, grammar masking sets invalid tokens' logits to $-\\infty$ at each decode step. For JSON-schema IE outputs, structural tokens (\\texttt{\\{}, \\texttt{\"entities\"}, \\texttt{:}, etc.) are often the \\emph{only} valid token at a given step, contributing $\\log p \\approx 0$. Only entity name and type tokens offer genuine choice. Consequently, the sequence-level log-probabilities of the $N$ candidates collapse to a narrow range.\n231\t\n232\tQuantitatively, across 529 NER instances, the median within-sample log-prob range (max $-$ min over $N = 8$ candidates) is just 0.031, and 60\\% of instances have range $< 0.05$. The candidates are effectively indistinguishable by log-prob, making ranking impossible regardless of the underlying quality variation.\n233\t\n234\t% ============================================================\n235\t\\subsection{Controlled Ablation: Free-Form Decoding}\n236\t\\label{sec:freeform}\n237\t\n238\tTo test whether constrained decoding \\emph{causes} the selection failure, we conduct a controlled ablation: identical model, data, $N$, and temperature, but without grammar constraints. Outputs are parsed post-hoc; 90\\% yield valid NER parses ($n = 476$) and 88\\% yield valid RE parses ($n = 350$).\n239\t\n240\t\\paragraph{Log-prob variance recovery.}\n241\tTable~\\ref{tab:freeform_main} shows the core result. Removing grammar constraints increases the median within-sample log-prob range by $10.3\\times$ (NER: $0.031 \\to 0.32$; Wilcoxon $p = 1.2 \\times 10^{-45}$). The fraction of instances with range $< 0.05$ drops from 60\\% to 8\\%.\n242\t\n243\t\\paragraph{Selection performance recovery.}\n244\tWith variance restored, log-prob selection achieves $+5.0$pp over greedy on NER ($p = 0.008$, Cohen's $d = 0.31$, 95\\% CI [$+1.8$, $+8.2$]pp) and $+3.2$pp on RE ($p = 0.032$). This captures 33.8\\% of the oracle headroom on NER and 16.5\\% on RE.\n245\t\n246\t\\begin{table}[t]\n247\t\\centering\n248\t\\caption{Constrained vs.\\ free-form decoding: log-prob behavior and selection (SciERC, Qwen3-8B). Free-form recovers log-prob variance and enables significant selection gains. This is the paper's central result.}\n249\t\\label{tab:freeform_main}\n250\t\\small\n251\t\\begin{tabular}{ll cccccc}\n252\t\\toprule\n253\tTask & Regime & $n$ & Range$_{\\text{med}}$ & \\%${<}0.05$ & $\\rho$ & Sel.\\ F1 & $\\Delta$pp \\\\\n254\t\\midrule\n255\t\\multirow{2}{*}{NER}\n256\t& Constrained & 529 & 0.031 & 60\\% & 0.205 & 0.650 & $+$0.6 \\\\\n257\t& Free-form   & 476 & 0.320 & 8\\%  & \\bmark{0.420} & \\bmark{0.694} & $+$\\bmark{5.0}$^{**}$ \\\\\n258\t\\midrule\n259\t\\multirow{2}{*}{RE}\n260\t& Constrained & 397 & 0.028 & --- & 0.266 & 0.410 & $+$0.7 \\\\\n261\t& Free-form   & 350 & 0.270 & --- & \\bmark{0.350} & \\bmark{0.436} & $+$\\bmark{3.2}$^{*}$ \\\\\n262\t\\bottomrule\n263\t\\multicolumn{8}{l}{\\small $^{**}p < 0.01$, $^{*}p < 0.05$. Range$_{\\text{med}}$: median within-sample log-prob range.} \\\\\n264\t\\end{tabular}\n265\t\\end{table}\n266\t\n267\t\\paragraph{Signal ranking reversal.}\n268\tTable~\\ref{tab:freeform_signals} compares all five signals under free-form decoding. The most striking finding is the complete reversal of log-prob's ranking position:\n269\t\\begin{itemize}\n270\t\\item \\textbf{Constrained}: LogProb $\\rho = 0.205$ (worst) $\\ll$ SJ $\\rho = 0.360$ $<$ VotConf $\\rho = 0.379$ (best)\n271\t\\item \\textbf{Free-form}: LogProb $\\rho = 0.420$ (best) $>$ VotConf $\\rho = 0.390$ $>$ SJ $\\rho = 0.370$\n272\t\\end{itemize}\n273\tThe same signal goes from worst to best simply by changing the decoding regime. Structural signals (SJ, VotConf) remain stable across regimes, confirming they are unaffected by log-prob compression---and confirming that the compression specifically targets log-prob.\n274\t\n275\t\\begin{table}[t]\n276\t\\centering\n277\t\\caption{Signal comparison under free-form decoding (SciERC NER, Qwen3-8B, $n = 476$). Log-prob reverses from worst (constrained, Table~\\ref{tab:qe_scierc}) to best. Structural signals remain stable.}\n278\t\\label{tab:freeform_signals}\n279\t\\small\n280\t\\begin{tabular}{l cc c cc}\n281\t\\toprule\n282\t& \\multicolumn{2}{c}{\\textbf{Free-form NER}} & & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{\\textbf{Free-form RE}} \\\\\n283\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-3} \\cmidrule(lr){5-6}\n284\tSignal & $\\rho$ & AUROC & & $\\rho$ & AUROC \\\\\n285\t\\midrule\n286\tLogProb & \\bmark{0.420} & 0.680 & & \\bmark{0.350} & 0.650 \\\\\n287\tVotConf & 0.390 & 0.690 & & 0.340 & 0.680 \\\\\n288\tSJ      & 0.370 & 0.700 & & 0.260 & --- \\\\\n289\tEM      & 0.300 & \\bmark{0.750} & & --- & --- \\\\\n290\tFK      & 0.280 & --- & & --- & --- \\\\\n291\t\\bottomrule\n292\t\\end{tabular}\n293\t\\end{table}\n294\t\n295\t% ============================================================\n296\t\\subsection{Supervised Verifier Baseline}\n297\t\\label{sec:verifier}\n298\t\n299\tTo establish an upper bound on selection under constrained decoding, we train a supervised DeBERTa-v3-large verifier (304M parameters) via 5-fold cross-validation. The verifier takes (sentence, model output) pairs and predicts F1 scores using MSE loss over 5 epochs.\n300\t\n301\tTable~\\ref{tab:verifier} shows that the verifier substantially outperforms unsupervised signals on quality estimation (NER: $\\rho = 0.52$ vs.\\ $0.379$; AUROC $= 0.82$ vs.\\ $0.749$), confirming the gap is closable with supervision. However, its selection gain under constrained decoding is modest: $+3.6$pp on NER, with 27.3\\% headroom capture.\n302\t\n303\t\\begin{table}[t]\n304\t\\centering\n305\t\\caption{Supervised verifier (DeBERTa-v3-large, 5-fold CV) vs.\\ unsupervised signals under constrained decoding (SciERC, Qwen3-8B). Verifier improves estimation but selection gains remain limited by candidate homogeneity.}\n306\t\\label{tab:verifier}\n307\t\\small\n308\t\\begin{tabular}{l cccc cc}\n309\t\\toprule\n310\t& \\multicolumn{4}{c}{\\textbf{Quality Estimation}} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{\\textbf{Selection}} \\\\\n311\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-5} \\cmidrule(lr){6-7}\n312\t& $\\rho$ & $\\tau$ & AUROC & ECE & F1 & $\\Delta$pp \\\\\n313\t\\midrule\n314\t\\multicolumn{7}{l}{\\emph{NER}} \\\\\n315\t\\quad DeBERTa Verifier & \\bmark{0.520} & \\bmark{0.390} & \\bmark{0.820} & \\bmark{0.180} & 0.680 & $+$3.6 \\\\\n316\t\\quad Best Unsup.\\ (VotConf) & 0.379 & --- & 0.684 & 0.381 & --- & --- \\\\\n317\t\\quad Best Unsup.\\ (EM, AUROC) & 0.295 & --- & 0.749 & 0.353 & --- & --- \\\\\n318\t\\midrule\n319\t\\multicolumn{7}{l}{\\emph{RE}} \\\\\n320\t\\quad DeBERTa Verifier & \\bmark{0.440} & \\bmark{0.330} & \\bmark{0.780} & --- & 0.423 & $+$1.9 \\\\\n321\t\\quad Best Unsup.\\ (VotConf) & 0.350 & --- & 0.694 & --- & --- & --- \\\\\n322\t\\bottomrule\n323\t\\end{tabular}\n324\t\\end{table}\n325\t\n326\t\\paragraph{Free-form log-prob outperforms the supervised verifier.}\n327\tTable~\\ref{tab:hierarchy} presents the complete gap-closure hierarchy. The zero-cost free-form log-prob ($+5.0$pp, 33.8\\% headroom) exceeds the supervised verifier operating on constrained outputs ($+3.6$pp, 27.3\\% headroom). This is the paper's strongest evidence that the bottleneck lies in candidate diversity (determined by the decoding regime), not in the quality of the scoring function. Even a trained verifier with access to labeled data cannot overcome the homogeneity of constrained-decoding candidates.\n328\t\n329\t\\begin{table}[t]\n330\t\\centering\n331\t\\caption{Selection gap-closure hierarchy (SciERC NER). Free-form log-prob (zero additional cost) outperforms the supervised verifier (requires labeled data and training), confirming that the bottleneck is candidate diversity, not scoring quality.}\n332\t\\label{tab:hierarchy}\n333\t\\small\n334\t\\begin{tabular}{l ccc}\n335\t\\toprule\n336\tStrategy & NER F1 & $\\Delta$ vs Greedy & \\% Headroom \\\\\n337\t\\midrule\n338\tRandom (constrained)              & 0.608 & $-3.6$pp & --- \\\\\n339\tGreedy                            & 0.644 & ---      & 0\\% \\\\\n340\tBest Unsupervised (constrained)   & 0.650 & $+0.6$pp & 4.5\\% \\\\\n341\tDeBERTa Verifier (constrained)    & 0.680 & $+3.6$pp & 27.3\\% \\\\\n342\tFree-form LogProb                 & \\bmark{0.694} & $+$\\bmark{5.0}pp & \\bmark{33.8}\\% \\\\\n343\t\\midrule\n344\tOracle (constrained)              & 0.776 & $+13.2$pp & --- \\\\\n345\tOracle (free-form)                & 0.792 & $+14.8$pp & --- \\\\\n346\t\\bottomrule\n347\t\\end{tabular}\n348\t\\end{table}\n349\t\n350\t% ============================================================\n351\t\\section{Discussion}\n352\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n353\t\n354\t\\paragraph{Why constrained decoding compresses log-prob.}\n355\tGrammar-guided decoding applies a token-level mask at each generation step, setting logits of grammar-invalid tokens to $-\\infty$. For JSON-schema IE, the majority of decode steps have a single valid continuation (structural tokens like brackets, colons, and key names). Only a small fraction of steps---those generating entity names and types---offer genuine vocabulary choice. The sequence-level log-prob, averaged over all steps, is dominated by the near-deterministic structural steps, washing out the quality-relevant variation in entity steps.\n356\t\n357\t\\paragraph{Why structural signals are unaffected.}\n358\tSJ, FK, EM, and VotConf operate on the \\emph{parsed output} (entity sets), not on token-level probabilities. They are invariant to the decoding regime because they compare extracted structures, not generation processes. Their stability across constrained and free-form regimes (SJ: $0.360 \\to 0.370$) serves as a natural control: the same model on the same data produces similar structural agreement regardless of how tokens are selected.\n359\t\n360\t\\paragraph{When would consistency-based selection help?}\n361\tOur negative result for structural signals is specific to the best-of-$N$ selection problem under fine-tuned models. Consistency-based confidence may outperform log-prob when: (a) the model is poorly calibrated, as in few-shot or in-context learning without fine-tuning; (b) log-probabilities are unavailable, as with closed-source APIs; (c) ensemble or mixture-of-expert settings where token-level scores are not comparable across components; or (d) tasks where the model is systematically overconfident and consistency provides an independent quality signal.\n362\t\n363\t\\paragraph{Practical implications.}\n364\tOur results suggest a concrete mitigation: generate candidates without grammar constraints, parse outputs post-hoc, and select among parseable candidates using log-prob. On our fine-tuned models, 90\\% of free-form outputs are parseable, and the 10\\% parse failure rate is a modest cost for $+5.0$pp selection gain. For deployment scenarios where 100\\% parse rate is required, a hybrid approach---free-form generation with constrained fallback for unparseable outputs---may capture most of the benefit.\n365\t\n366\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n367\t(1) Our experiments use two 8B-parameter models; larger models may exhibit different compression patterns. (2) We evaluate on two NER datasets and one RE dataset; informal-text domains (social media, clinical notes) are not covered. (3) The free-form parse rate of 90\\% may be lower for less fine-tuned models. (4) We study sampling at $T = 1.0$; the interaction between temperature and constrained decoding deserves further investigation.\n368\t\n369\t% ============================================================\n370\t\\section{Conclusion}\n371\t\n372\tWe identified and experimentally verified a regime-specific selection bottleneck in structured information extraction: constrained (grammar-guided) decoding compresses log-probability scores, destroying the only per-sample ranking signal and preventing effective best-of-$N$ selection despite strong quality estimation performance (AUROC up to 0.75). A controlled free-form ablation confirmed the causal mechanism---removing grammar constraints recovered log-prob variance by $10\\times$ and enabled significant NER selection gains of $+5.0$pp ($p = 0.008$). The signal ranking reversed entirely, with log-prob going from worst to best, providing direct evidence that constrained decoding is the cause. A supervised verifier achieved only $+3.6$pp under constrained decoding, below the zero-cost free-form log-prob gain, confirming the bottleneck lies in candidate diversity rather than scoring quality. These findings suggest that relaxed decoding with post-hoc validation is a more promising direction than stronger scoring functions for test-time scaling in structured IE.\n373\t\n374\t% ============================================================\n375\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n376\t\n377\t\\begin{thebibliography}{40}\n378\t\n379\t\\bibitem[Wang et~al.(2023)]{wang2023selfconsistency}\n380\tWang, X., Wei, J., Schuurmans, D., Le, Q., Chi, E., Narang, S., Chowdhery, A., and Zhou, D.\n381\t\\newblock Self-consistency improves chain of thought reasoning in language models.\n382\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2023.\n383\t\n384\t\\bibitem[Eikema and Aziz(2022)]{eikema2022sampling}\n385\tEikema, B. and Aziz, W.\n386\t\\newblock Sampling-based approximations to minimum {B}ayes risk decoding for neural machine translation.\n387\t\\newblock In \\emph{EACL}, 2022.\n388\t\n389\t\\bibitem[Willard and Louf(2023)]{willard2023efficient}\n390\tWillard, B.~T. and Louf, R.\n391\t\\newblock Efficient guided generation for large language models.\n392\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2307.09702}, 2023.\n393\t\n394\t\\bibitem[Cobbe et~al.(2021)]{cobbe2021training}\n395\tCobbe, K., Kosaraju, V., Bavarian, M., Chen, M., Jun, H., Kaiser, L., Plappert, M., Tworek, J., Hilton, J., Nakano, R., Hesse, C., and Schulman, J.\n396\t\\newblock Training verifiers to solve math word problems.\n397\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2110.14168}, 2021.\n398\t\n399\t\\bibitem[Lightman et~al.(2023)]{lightman2023lets}\n400\tLightman, H., Kosaraju, V., Burda, Y., Edwards, H., Baker, B., Lee, T., Leike, J., Schulman, J., Sutskever, I., and Cobbe, K.\n401\t\\newblock Let's verify step by step.\n402\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2024.\n403\t\n404\t\\bibitem[Kuhn et~al.(2023)]{kuhn2023semantic}\n405\tKuhn, L., Gal, Y., and Farquhar, S.\n406\t\\newblock Semantic uncertainty: Linguistic invariances for uncertainty estimation in natural language generation.\n407\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2023.\n408\t\n409\t\\bibitem[Manakul et~al.(2023)]{manakul2023selfcheckgpt}\n410\tManakul, P., Liusie, A., and Gales, M.~J.~F.\n411\t\\newblock {SelfCheckGPT}: Zero-resource black-box hallucination detection for generative large language models.\n412\t\\newblock In \\emph{EMNLP}, 2023.\n413\t\n414\t\\bibitem[Guo et~al.(2017)]{guo2017calibration}\n415\tGuo, C., Pleiss, G., Sun, Y., and Weinberger, K.~Q.\n416\t\\newblock On calibration of modern neural networks.\n417\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICML}, 2017.\n418\t\n419\t\\bibitem[Geifman and El-Yaniv(2017)]{geifman2017selective}\n420\tGeifman, Y. and El-Yaniv, R.\n421\t\\newblock Selective classification for deep neural networks.\n422\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2017.\n423\t\n424\t\\bibitem[Gal and Ghahramani(2016)]{gal2016dropout}\n425\tGal, Y. and Ghahramani, Z.\n426\t\\newblock Dropout as a {B}ayesian approximation: Representing model uncertainty in deep learning.\n427\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICML}, 2016.\n428\t\n429\t\\bibitem[Lakshminarayanan et~al.(2017)]{lakshminarayanan2017simple}\n430\tLakshminarayanan, B., Pritzel, A., and Blundell, C.\n431\t\\newblock Simple and scalable predictive uncertainty estimation using deep ensembles.\n432\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2017.\n433\t\n434\t\\bibitem[Xiao and Wang(2019)]{xiao2019quantifying}\n435\tXiao, Y. and Wang, W.~Y.\n436\t\\newblock Quantifying uncertainties in natural language processing tasks.\n437\t\\newblock In \\emph{AAAI}, 2019.\n438\t\n439\t\\bibitem[Shelmanov et~al.(2021)]{shelmanov2021certain}\n440\tShelmanov, A., Tsymbalov, E., Puzyrev, D., Fedyanin, K., Panchenko, A., and Panov, M.\n441\t\\newblock How certain is your {T}ransformer?\n442\t\\newblock In \\emph{EACL}, 2021.\n443\t\n444\t\\bibitem[He et~al.(2020)]{he2020towards}\n445\tHe, J., Zhang, Z., Berg-Kirkpatrick, T., and Neubig, G.\n446\t\\newblock Towards better uncertainty estimation for NER.\n447\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2012.12954}, 2020.\n448\t\n449\t\\bibitem[Kuleshov and Liang(2015)]{kuleshov2015calibration}\n450\tKuleshov, V. and Liang, P.\n451\t\\newblock Calibrated structured prediction.\n452\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2015.\n453\t\n454\t\\bibitem[Jagannatha and Yu(2022)]{jagannatha2022calibrating}\n455\tJagannatha, A.~N. and Yu, H.\n456\t\\newblock Calibrating structured output predictors for natural language processing.\n457\t\\newblock In \\emph{ACL}, 2022.\n458\t\n459\t\\bibitem[Snell et~al.(2024)]{snell2024scaling}\n460\tSnell, C., Lee, J., Xu, K., and Kumar, A.\n461\t\\newblock Scaling {LLM} test-time compute optimally can be more effective than scaling model parameters.\n462\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2408.03314}, 2024.\n463\t\n464\t\\bibitem[Brown et~al.(2024)]{brown2024large}\n465\tBrown, B., Juravsky, J., Ehrlich, R., Clark, R., Le, Q.~V., R{\\'e}, C., and Mirhoseini, A.\n466\t\\newblock Large language monkeys: Scaling inference compute with repeated sampling.\n467\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2407.21787}, 2024.\n468\t\n469\t\\bibitem[Luan et~al.(2018)]{luan2018multi}\n470\tLuan, Y., He, L., Ostendorf, M., and Hajishirzi, H.\n471\t\\newblock Multi-task identification of entities, relations, and coreference for scientific knowledge graph construction.\n472\t\\newblock In \\emph{EMNLP}, 2018.\n473\t\n474\t\\bibitem[Tjong Kim~Sang and De~Meulder(2003)]{tjong2003conll}\n475\tTjong Kim~Sang, E.~F. and De~Meulder, F.\n476\t\\newblock Introduction to the {CoNLL}-2003 shared task: Language-independent named entity recognition.\n477\t\\newblock In \\emph{CoNLL}, 2003.\n478\t\n479\t\\bibitem[N{\\'e}dellec and Xia(2026)]{nedellec2026consistency}\n480\tN{\\'e}dellec, C. and Xia, R.\n481\t\\newblock Consistency--accuracy correlation in hard-prompted {LLM}s for entity and relation extraction.\n482\t\\newblock \\emph{Genomics Informatics}, 2026.\n483\t\n484\t\\bibitem[{UQ Survey}(2025)]{uqsurvey2025}\n485\tGeng, J., et~al.\n486\t\\newblock A survey of uncertainty quantification in large language models.\n487\t\\newblock In \\emph{ACL Findings}, 2025.\n488\t\n489\t\\bibitem[{UQ+Calibration Survey}(2025)]{uqcalibsurvey2025}\n490\tLi, Y., et~al.\n491\t\\newblock A survey on uncertainty quantification and calibration of large language models.\n492\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2503.15850}, 2025.\n493\t\n494\t\\bibitem[{CISC}(2025)]{cisc2025}\n495\tChen, W., et~al.\n496\t\\newblock Confidence-weighted self-consistency for reasoning.\n497\t\\newblock In \\emph{ACL Findings}, 2025.\n498\t\n499\t\\bibitem[{Self-Certainty}(2026)]{selfcertainty2026}\n500\tLi, H., et~al.\n501\t\\newblock Self-certainty: Reward-free best-of-{N} sampling via {KL} divergence.\n502\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2026.\n503\t\n504\t\\bibitem[{ReASC}(2026)]{reasc2026}\n505\tZhang, T., et~al.\n506\t\\newblock Reliability-aware adaptive self-consistency.\n507\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2601.02970}, 2026.\n508\t\n509\t\\bibitem[{Ranked Voting SC}(2025)]{rankedvoting2025}\n510\tKim, S., et~al.\n511\t\\newblock Ranked voting self-consistency.\n512\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2505.10772}, 2025.\n513\t\n514\t\\bibitem[{Atomic SC}(2024)]{atomicsc2024}\n515\tDhuliawala, S., et~al.\n516\t\\newblock Atomic self-consistency for better long-form generations.\n517\t\\newblock In \\emph{EMNLP}, 2024.\n518\t\n519\t\\bibitem[{SE Probes}(2024)]{seprobes2024}\n520\tKossen, J., et~al.\n521\t\\newblock Semantic entropy probes: Robust and cheap hallucination detection in {LLM}s.\n522\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2406.15927}, 2024.\n523\t\n524\t\\bibitem[{Latent SC}(2025)]{latentsc2025}\n525\tChen, H., et~al.\n526\t\\newblock Latent self-consistency.\n527\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2508.18395}, 2025.\n528\t\n529\t\\bibitem[{Abstention Survey}(2025)]{abstentionsurvey2025}\n530\tFeng, S., et~al.\n531\t\\newblock Know your limits: A survey on abstention in large language models.\n532\t\\newblock \\emph{TACL}, 2025.\n533\t\n534\t\\bibitem[{Conf.\\ Est.\\ Survey}(2024)]{confestsurvey2024}\n535\tXiong, M., et~al.\n536\t\\newblock Can {LLM}s express their uncertainty? An empirical evaluation of confidence elicitation in {LLM}s.\n537\t\\newblock In \\emph{NAACL}, 2024.\n538\t\n539\t\\bibitem[Vashurin et~al.(2025a)]{cocoa2025}\n540\tVashurin, R., Tsvigun, A., and Shelmanov, A.\n541\t\\newblock {CoCoA}: Combining confidence and consistency for uncertainty quantification.\n542\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2025.\n543\t\n544\t\\bibitem[Vashurin et~al.(2025b)]{vashurin2025systematic}\n545\tVashurin, R., et~al.\n546\t\\newblock Systematic evaluation of uncertainty quantification methods for large language models.\n547\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2510.20460}, 2025.\n548\t\n549\t\\bibitem[Qwen(2024)]{qwen2024}\n550\tQwen Team.\n551\t\\newblock Qwen3 technical report.\n552\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2505.09388}, 2025.\n553\t\n554\t\\bibitem[Dubey et~al.(2024)]{llama31}\n555\tDubey, A., et~al.\n556\t\\newblock The {L}lama 3 herd of models.\n557\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2407.21783}, 2024.\n558\t\n559\t\\bibitem[Culotta and McCallum(2004)]{culotta2004confidence}\n560\tCulotta, A. and McCallum, A.\n561\t\\newblock Confidence estimation for information extraction.\n562\t\\newblock In \\emph{HLT-NAACL}, 2004.\n563\t\n564\t\\end{thebibliography}\n565\t\n566\t% ============================================================\n567\t\\appendix\n568\t\\section{Out-of-Domain Robustness}\n569\t\\label{app:ood}\n570\t\n571\tTo test whether the constrained decoding bottleneck persists under domain shift, we evaluate SciERC-fine-tuned Qwen3-8B on WNUT-17 (zero-shot cross-domain NER, $n = 1{,}287$). Table~\\ref{tab:ood} shows results.\n572\t\n573\tPerformance drops substantially (greedy F1: $0.32$ vs.\\ in-domain $0.64$), and 58\\% of samples produce F1 $= 0$ due to schema mismatch. The bottleneck pattern persists: log-prob $\\rho = -0.08$ (negative, indicating overconfidence on familiar patterns), while structural signals remain weakly positive (VotConf $\\rho = 0.18$, SJ $\\rho = 0.15$). Log-prob compression is more severe (range median $= 0.02$), consistent with the constrained decoding mechanism. This rules out the alternative hypothesis that the bottleneck is specific to in-domain settings.\n574\t\n575\t\\begin{table}[h]\n576\t\\centering\n577\t\\caption{OOD signal robustness (SciERC $\\to$ WNUT-17, Qwen3-8B, constrained, $N\\!=\\!8$). Bottleneck pattern persists: log-prob negative, structural signals weakly positive.}\n578\t\\label{tab:ood}\n579\t\\small\n580\t\\begin{tabular}{l cc}\n581\t\\toprule\n582\tSignal & $\\rho$ & AUROC \\\\\n583\t\\midrule\n584\tVotConf & \\bmark{0.180} & \\bmark{0.600} \\\\\n585\tSJ      & 0.150 & 0.580 \\\\\n586\tFK      & 0.120 & 0.560 \\\\\n587\tEM      & 0.110 & 0.570 \\\\\n588\tLogProb & $-$0.080 & 0.480 \\\\\n589\t\\midrule\n590\t\\multicolumn{3}{l}{\\small Greedy F1: 0.32, Oracle F1: 0.52} \\\\\n591\t\\multicolumn{3}{l}{\\small 58\\% samples F1 $= 0$, logprob range median $= 0.02$} \\\\\n592\t\\bottomrule\n593\t\\end{tabular}\n594\t\\end{table}\n595\t\n596\t\\end{document}\n597\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01GJcwQEcAFdZugRUsDbxRDD","session_id":"8860f592-6591-47fa-b978-f5709a359b4d","uuid":"839a2f51-5905-4996-9cd6-d502bcde8e6e","timestamp":"2026-05-15T10:08:35.979Z"}
